Tier A — Must-Have (MVP Core)
Without these, the client-only application cannot function.
Direct Client-to-API Menu Fetcher: Fetches raw menu JSON directly from Wolt's internal menu endpoint using a restaurant slug, handling network requests natively on-device.
Client-Side OpenRouter LLM Analysis Engine: Directly sends batches of dish titles, descriptions, and modifier options from the client to the OpenRouter API (e.g., running cost-effective, high-reasoning models like Claude 3.5 Haiku, DeepSeek, or Gemini 1.5 Flash). The prompt instructs the model to return structured JSON with keto safety evaluations and specific waiter modification scripts.
Client API Key Configuration: A secure local storage input (e.g., Flutter Secure Storage) allowing the user to provide or store an OpenRouter API key on-device.
Traffic-Light Tagging (🟢 / 🟡 / 🔴): Renders the structured LLM response into clear visual categories:
🟢 Green: Safe as-is (low-carb, no starchy sides, no added sugars).
🟡 Yellow: Safe with modifications (compliant core protein/salad paired with high-carb sides or dressings).
🔴 Red / Filtered: Incompatible base (pasta, pizza, risotto, grain bowls, deep-fried breading).
LLM-Generated Waiter Instructions: Expandable cards on 🟡 Yellow items displaying specific, actionable phrases generated by the model for servers (e.g., "Swap potato purée for leafy greens; oil and vinegar on the side").
Direct Venue Identifier Input: Text input field to paste a restaurant URL or slug to trigger the fetch-and-analyze loop immediately without external directory dependencies.
Tier B — Necessary
Essential for a smooth, practical user experience on mobile.
Local LLM Response Caching: Local persistent storage (Hive or SQLite) that caches analyzed menus by restaurant slug and dish ID, preventing redundant OpenRouter token consumption and network latency on revisited venues.
Client-Side Venue Search: Direct queries to Wolt's public geocoding/autocomplete search endpoint to find local venues by name, address, or neighborhood.
Keto Menu Filtering & Sorting: UI controls to view only 🟢 Green, 🟢 + 🟡, or completely hide 🔴 Red items.
Full-Screen "Waiter Card" & Quick Copy: One-tap modal enlarging the LLM's waiter script to high-contrast text to present to waitstaff, plus a quick copy-to-clipboard button.
Tier C — Good to Have (Not Essential)
Improves flexibility and resilience without delaying core delivery.
Custom Dietary System Prompt Toggles: Client settings allowing users to inject custom constraints into the OpenRouter system prompt (e.g., "Strict Seed-Oil Free", "Dairy-Free Keto", or "Carnivore Only").
Local Heuristic Fallback: Lightweight on-device regex to categorize obvious dishes if the user runs out of OpenRouter credits or has no internet connection.
Secondary Platform Ingestion (10bis): Client parsing for 10bis numeric restaurant IDs formatted into the same OpenRouter prompt structure.
Personal Notes: Local notes attached to analyzed dishes (e.g., "Waitstaff happily substituted cauliflower").
Tier D — Nice to Have (Future Expansions)
Long-term enhancements for future iterations.
On-Device OCR to OpenRouter: Capturing physical paper menus via camera, running text extraction via ML Kit, and piping the raw text to OpenRouter for live analysis.
Shared Community Proxy & Global Cache: An optional lightweight backend that pools common OpenRouter analyses across users, reducing individual API billing.
Crowdsourced Modification Feedback: Community tagging indicating how accommodating the venue was with requested changes.
Shareable Menu Cards: Exporting an image or text summary of safe dishes to share via messaging apps.
